WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.660
A radio galaxy from nearly 12.5 billion years ago has been confirmed as one of the most powerful ever detected. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 216. Follow-up spectroscopy placed TXS-2354 plus 015 at a redshift of 4.946. The source was first flagged by its characteristic drop in optical light. The paper was posted to ARXIV on September 23rd.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.280
The Galaxy's radio eminition indicates an extremely luminous central engine.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.860
Observations came from multiple ground-based telescopes.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.660
Next, we turn to a major new chip fabrication effort.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:47.980
Elon Musk stated that Tesla and SpaceX will build and operate the planned TeraFab chip factory in Texas.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.700
Taiwan's semiconductor manufacturing company may subly space but will not take control.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.940
The facility aims to produce chips for one terawatt of computing power annually.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.780
Those chips will support optimist robots, cybercab vehicles and orbital data centers.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.100
Intel remains involved in design and packaging work for the project.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.700
The clarification ended speculation that began the previous week about external operators.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:18.620
While private industry scales computing for space ambitions,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.500
Europe is weighing its own next decade exploration strategy.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:28.000
European Space Agency officials described the current moment as potentially historic during

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.300
talks with member states.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.840
Discussions focused on exploration plans ahead of December's intermediate ministerial meeting.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.140
The agency highlighted ongoing studies for missions to the moon, Mars, and beyond.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:45.440
Member states reviewed technology roadmaps and funding priorities for the coming decade.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.260
Across the Atlantic, another company is quietly advancing its own crude spacecraft alongside

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:52.900
alongside larger launch programs.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.960
Blue Origin has maintained an internal team

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.820
of several dozen people on its space vehicle project

00:01:58.820 --> 00:01:59.820
for several years.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.580
Wind tunnel testing at NASA Langley

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.880
and Arcjet testing at NASA Ames

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.060
are scheduled this month.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.240
Parachute tests and additional hardware work

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.160
are also underway.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.080
The effort runs in parallel

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.360
with other company launch vehicle programs.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.620
While Blue Origin tests hardware,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.960
SpaceX is preparing a historic Florida launch site

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:26.560
for Starship. SpaceX reports strong progress at Kennedy Space Center's PAD 39A, ahead of

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.940
the first Starship orbital attempt from Florida. The company is completing infrastructure upgrades

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:37.700
at the historic Apollo era site. Engineers have focused on propellant loading systems and ground

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.780
support equipment. The PAD previously supported space shuttle and Falcon Heavy missions.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:48.200
From launch site engineering to a surprising classroom discovery about galaxies that stopped

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:53.460
forming stars. A UC Berkeley Astrophysics student catalogued three galaxies now

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:59.620
named Shapiro-Dwarf galaxies 1, 2 and 3. Each lies far from other galaxies yet

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.560
shows no ongoing star formation. The finding supplies direct evidence for a

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.320
longstanding theory about quenching in isolated systems. The work appears in

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:14.040
the Astrophysical Journal. The student analyzed archival survey data to locate

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:20.020
the objects. From quiet galaxies to a rare view of a star actively consuming its brown dwarf

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:25.480
companion. Astronomers captured the interaction between the star and its brown dwarf companion.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:31.600
The event provides a rare view of mass transfer outside the solar system. The brown dwarf is

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.560
losing material to the host star through Rochlobe overflow. Observations were made with ground-based

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:43.000
optical and infrared instruments. Shifting from stellar interactions to atmospheric mysteries

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:49.060
closer to home on Mars. Scientists combine Mars express observations with a meteorological model

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:55.280
to examine the clouds. The study points to homogeneous ice nucleation from water vapor as a possible

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:02.000
mechanism. The paper appears in nature geoscience. Elongated cloud structures on Mars have puzzled

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.880
researchers for years. The new analysis offers a physical explanation tied to atmospheric conditions.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:17.060
The confirmation of TXS-2354 plus 015 at Redshift 4.946 places it among the earliest

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:18.300
known radio galaxies.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:24.320
Its extreme power output at such distance implies rapid black hole growth shortly after

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:24.940
the Big Bang.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:30.120
The source was first identified through a characteristic drop in optical light before

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.100
spectroscopy locked in its distance.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:32.260
points.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.240
Follow-up work will test whether similar objects exist at even higher red shifts.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.080
This detection adds a concrete data point to models of early supermassive black hole formation.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.560
The galaxy's radio luminosity exceeds that of most known high-red shift sources by a significant

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.020
margin.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:56.020
Point a city-sized radio dish on Earth toward a distant pulsar while our planet races

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.480
around the sun at 30 kilometers per second.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:06.400
The distance between the telescope and the solar system's center of mass changes by up to 150 million kilometers over a year.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:12.240
That motion stretches or compresses the arrival time of each radio pulse by several milliseconds.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:21.100
Correcting for this effect requires shifting every measurement into a common reference frame called TDB, or barricentric dynamical time.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:27.060
Without that step, a perfectly steady pulsar appears to speed up and slow down once per orbit.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:33.000
The correction itself rests on general relativity because clocks run slightly faster or slower,

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.480
depending on their position in the sun's gravity well. We still lack a complete map of how these

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.380
timing residuals behave across the full sky at the nanosecond level that next generation

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:50.260
are raised demand. Acclaimed author and futurist David Brinn will join host Ashton Zeth on October 13th

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:55.700
at 6 p.m. Pacific time. The discussion will cover Mars exploration and related topics.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:00.180
Brin has written extensively on space settlement and technology futures.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.000
The event is open to the public through the Mar Society platform.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.460
Three University of Waterloo co-op alumni describe routes from campus into roles

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.760
at Canadian space companies and agencies. The stories highlight practical

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.140
experience gained through the university's placement program. Participants

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:22.680
worked on satellite subsystems, mission planning, and data analysis projects.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.340
The program connects students with employers across the national space industry.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:34.760
An April 6, 2026 screenshot shows handwritten notes on a tablet image of a dark moon eclipsing the sun.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:38.960
The image forms part of the mission's preliminary science documentation.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:43.380
Crew members recorded timing and visual details during the observation.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.840
The data contribute to studies of lunar lighting conditions.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:53.360
The Crab Nebula, catalogued as M1, is a supernova remnant visible in small telescopes.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.420
It formed from the 1054 supernova that briefly outshorn Venus.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.400
The remnant continues to emit across radio, optical, and x-ray wavelengths.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:07.860
Observers can locate it near the star's Zeta Tori on winter evenings.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:13.880
In June, the vehicle released a small satellite and later recaptured it, likely using a robotic

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:14.280
arm.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:19.880
In August, it deployed another satellite, moved several thousand miles away, then returned.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.160
The sequence demonstrates repeated on-orbit servicing capabilities.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.740
Analysts track the maneuvers through public orbital data.

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:34.480
The preliminary Lunar Science Report includes nearly 1,000 new images and 521 minutes of

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:35.100
crew audio.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.660
The data package supports ongoing analysis of the mission's lunar encounter.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.420
teams are processing the files for distribution to the broader research community.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.400
The release marks the first major science output from the crude flight.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:53.360
The scientists will contribute to efforts expected to discover thousands of new worlds.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.040
The Nancy Grace Roman telescopes wide field observations will map planetary populations across the galaxy.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:05.860
The researcher brings expertise in statistical methods for transit and microlens in detections.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.980
The mission is scheduled for launch later this decade.

